+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+## Scope and method
+
+This review covers the Spring Boot API and its configured integrations: identity/JWT
+authentication, the accounts and support APIs, payment dispatch, the scheme gateway,
+fraud screening, core ledger, notifications, scheduled payment processing, and the
+persistence configuration. It is based on the implementation and configuration in this
+repository, not on production telemetry or infrastructure controls that are not present
+here.
+
+Scores use the conventional 1–10 FMEA scale: Severity (S) is 1 = negligible and 10 =
+customer-money loss, cross-tenant disclosure, or prolonged regulatory/business impact;
+Occurrence (O) is 1 = unlikely and 10 = frequent or easy to trigger; Detection (D) is
+1 = almost certain to detect before customer impact and 10 = unlikely to detect before
+impact. The risk priority number is `S × O × D`. RPN is a prioritisation aid, not a
+substitute for judgement: any S=10 item requires treatment even if its RPN is modest.
+
+## Failure-mode register
+
+| ID | Failure mode | Likely cause | Effect on customers | Effect on dependent systems and teams | Detection / controls | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| F-01 | A payment is accepted by the scheme but remains `POSTING_FAILED`, or is posted twice | `PaymentDispatcher` submits to the scheme, then debits the local account and posts to the ledger. A timeout is treated as failure even though the ledger may have committed; there is no idempotent reconciliation/retry protocol. A scheme timeout has no recovery state. | The payment may be sent while the account view is wrong, or the customer may be charged twice; balance, payment status and statement can disagree. | Ledger, finance and reconciliation teams must investigate unmatched postings; scheme retries can create duplicate external instructions; support cannot give a reliable status. | Alert on `POSTING_FAILED`, compare scheme acknowledgements and ledger postings using the payment reference, and run daily balance/payment reconciliation. Current code only logs a warning for ledger timeout and has no metric or repair workflow. | 10 | 5 | 8 | 400 | Make scheme and ledger calls idempotent with durable correlation keys (`P{id}` / ledger reference); use an explicit unknown/repair state, query-before-retry, transactional outbox, and reconciliation queue. Never infer “not posted” from a timeout. Add failure-injection tests for accepted-before-timeout and committed-before-timeout cases. |
+| F-02 | Payment is sent without fraud screening | A fraud client and test fake exist, but no production payment path calls `FraudScreeningClient`; `HELD_FOR_REVIEW` is unused. | Fraudulent payments can leave the account; legitimate customers may bear loss and reimbursement delays. | Fraud operations receive no screening event, and downstream scheme/ledger activity proceeds without the expected control evidence. | Monitor the ratio of payments with a screening decision (currently effectively zero), audit every payment for a decision and alert on missing decisions. | 10 | 6 | 9 | 540 | Insert mandatory screening before scheme submission. Define fail-closed behavior for `REFER`/unavailable, persist the decision and model/version, and only allow an audited override by authorised staff. |
+| F-03 | Queued payment is never dispatched or is dispatched more than once | The only worker is an in-process `@Scheduled` job at 06:00 on weekdays. There is no durable job claim/lock, retry/backoff, dead-letter state, or scheduler health signal; a crash or multi-instance deployment can skip or duplicate work. | A payment misses its promised execution date or is sent twice; customers may incur late fees or lose trust. | Scheme traffic and ledger entries diverge from the payment table; support and operations must manually replay or cancel work. | Gauge due `QUEUED` age and count, alert when due work remains after 06:00, and correlate each dispatch with an idempotency key. No such operational metrics are implemented. | 9 | 6 | 8 | 432 | Replace the in-process loop with a durable queue/outbox and worker lease; make dispatch idempotent, retry transient failures with backoff, dead-letter permanent failures, and expose job lag/last-success metrics and an operator replay tool. |
+| F-04 | Local balance and daily-limit checks allow overspending under concurrency | `create` reads the balance and today’s payments before saving; there is no row lock, optimistic version, atomic reservation, or database constraint. Concurrent requests can both pass the checks. | Customers can spend more than the available balance or daily limit; later payments fail or the account requires correction. | Ledger rejects or receives an overdrawn sequence; finance and support need manual adjustments and customers may be compensated. | Load tests with concurrent payments, compare local reservations to ledger debits, and alert on negative balances or daily-limit breaches. | 9 | 5 | 8 | 360 | Use an atomic funds reservation/ledger-authoritative balance check with locking or versioning, enforce the daily limit in the same transaction, and add concurrency tests and invariant alerts. |
+| F-05 | Payment request is persisted but the synchronous dispatch fails with an unhandled exception | Scheme timeout/rejection types are not uniformly handled by `PaymentService`; a timeout can escape after a `RECEIVED` row was saved. HTTP 5xx does not identify whether the external side accepted the instruction. | Customer sees an error and may retry, creating duplicate or ambiguous payments; the original can still execute. | Operations see stranded `RECEIVED` rows and uncertain scheme state; support cannot distinguish retry-safe from already-sent. | Alert on old `RECEIVED` rows, trace request-to-scheme correlation, and inspect 5xx rates. There is no recovery state transition for this path. | 9 | 5 | 8 | 360 | Treat submission as an asynchronous state machine, persist an idempotency key supplied by the client, classify timeout as unknown, and provide status polling/reconciliation rather than asking customers to retry blindly. |
+| F-06 | Notification outage is hidden or customers are told the wrong payment state | Notifications have no configured timeout, retry, queue, or delivery status. The dispatcher catches all runtime failures and only logs a warning after payment processing. | Payment may complete with no confirmation; or a notification can be delayed/duplicated after a retry. Customers and support may act on stale information. | Notification and customer-service teams have no durable event to replay; incident impact is visible only through complaints. | Track notification send success/failure and age, use provider delivery callbacks, and alert on dropped events. Current detection is a warning log only. | 6 | 7 | 9 | 378 | Publish a durable `PaymentCompleted` event/outbox after the payment state is known; add bounded retries, idempotent message IDs, provider timeouts, a dead-letter queue and delivery metrics. |
+| F-07 | Service loses all data or resets unexpectedly | `application.yml` configures `jdbc:h2:mem:accounts`, `DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1`, `ddl-auto: create-drop`, and always runs `data.sql`. This is suitable for tests but not durable production storage. | Accounts, payments, cases and audit history disappear on restart; customers cannot access trustworthy balances or payment evidence. | Ledger/scheme records no longer reconcile to the service; incident response, finance, compliance and support lose the local source of workflow state. | Startup configuration review and a restart/data-persistence smoke test; alert on schema recreation and unexpected row-count changes. | 10 | 3 | 10 | 300 | Use a production-managed durable database, migrations (`ddl-auto=validate`), backups and restore drills. Fail startup when a production profile points to H2 or fixture initialisation. |
+| F-08 | Cross-tenant or over-broad data access | Tenant and role values are trusted from JWT claims; resource checks are spread across services. A future endpoint/repository query can omit a tenant predicate, and the payment `get` path relies on account visibility rather than a tenant-scoped payment query. | A customer or staff member can see another bank’s balances, transactions, payment details, or internal information; this is a confidentiality and regulatory incident. | Other banks, identity/security teams, compliance, and support are affected; incident response may require tenant-wide notification and access review. | Negative integration tests for every endpoint using same IDs across tenants, authorization decision logs, and alerts on anomalous tenant access. Existing tests cover several access cases but not systematic query-level isolation. | 10 | 3 | 8 | 240 | Make tenant scoping mandatory in repositories and service methods, bind resource tenant to the authenticated principal, add database row-level isolation where appropriate, and maintain a deny-by-default endpoint/access matrix with cross-tenant tests. |
+| F-09 | Privilege escalation through malformed or over-privileged JWT claims | `JwtClaimsConverter` maps arbitrary `roles` and `scope` claims from a signature-valid token. The service does not validate issuer, audience, token freshness, tenant membership, role combinations, or scope semantics in this code. | A compromised/misissued token can expose staff data or initiate payments/closure actions; customer and internal data can be changed or disclosed. | Identity, security operations, compliance and all tenant banks face a high-severity access-control incident. | Identity-provider token validation, issuer/audience checks, short-lived tokens, authentication failure and privileged-action audit logs, and automated tests for role/scope combinations. | 10 | 3 | 8 | 240 | Configure issuer/audience and required claims, validate tenant/user membership against authoritative identity data, use least-privilege scopes, and audit/admin-review all privileged actions. Add security regression tests for forged claims and mixed roles. |
+| F-10 | A support or customer workflow is unavailable or silently incomplete | Case and closure models have read/create support only; there is no controller to add comments, assign/resolve cases, or approve/reject a closure. Closure requests can remain `PENDING` indefinitely. | Customers cannot receive a resolution or close an account; closure and support requests age without a clear status. | Support, approvers and operations must use out-of-band tools; downstream account lifecycle and compliance processes do not receive completion signals. | Dashboards for open/unassigned cases and pending closure age; SLA alerts and endpoint contract tests. Current code provides no SLA worker or escalation. | 7 | 6 | 8 | 336 | Implement the complete state transitions with role/tenant checks, audit actor/time/reason, and publish lifecycle events. Until then, document the capability gap and alert on aged pending items. |
+| F-11 | Incorrect execution date around holidays, timezone or cutoff | Working days are hard-coded as Monday–Friday; the clock is UK time and the scheme cutoff is configured as a local time. Bank holidays, clock changes, scheme calendar differences, and boundary precision are not modelled. | Payments may execute on a non-banking day or one day late, causing missed bills, fees, or customer complaints. | Scheme rejects or holds the instruction; support and payment-operations teams must explain and repair dates. | Contract tests around 15:30, DST, UK bank holidays and scheme calendar; compare requested, scheduled and scheme execution dates. | 7 | 5 | 7 | 245 | Use an authoritative bank/scheme holiday calendar and explicit timezone/instant policy; test cutoff inclusivity and DST, and expose the calculated execution date to the customer before submission. |
+| F-12 | Sensitive operational data or payment identifiers leak through logs/errors | Payment and integration exceptions are not normalised; notification failures and ledger failures log payment IDs with stack traces, while HTTP clients and framework errors may expose upstream details. | Attackers or unauthorised operators may learn payment/account information; customers may receive inconsistent error details. | Security/compliance must investigate log access and rotate credentials or identifiers; monitoring may also be polluted by high-cardinality data. | Central redaction tests, log scanning/DLP rules, structured audit logs, and review of 4xx/5xx response bodies. | 8 | 4 | 8 | 256 | Define a safe error contract, redact account/payee data and upstream response bodies, use structured correlation IDs instead of sensitive values, and restrict/audit log access. |
+| F-13 | Database/schema/fixture startup failure or partial migration | Schema is generated at startup and `data.sql` is always executed; production-like schema evolution, concurrent startup, bad fixture data, or an unavailable database can prevent boot. | API is unavailable, including account reads and payment status; requests fail during a release or restart. | Deployment rolls back or dependent channels receive errors; release, database and support teams are paged. | Readiness probe that includes database/schema checks, startup failure alerting, migration rehearsal, and release smoke tests. | 8 | 4 | 3 | 96 | Separate migrations from application startup, version and rehearse schema changes, use readiness (not liveness) for dependencies, and keep fixture loading test-profile-only. |
+| F-14 | External integration contract or configuration failure | URLs and timeout properties are static configuration; clients assume exact response fields/statuses. Scheme handles only HTTP 4xx as rejection, ledger handles only `ResourceAccessException`, and notification has no timeout. | Payments may fail, hang, or be marked incorrectly; account servicing and confirmations are delayed. | Scheme/ledger/fraud/notification teams receive malformed traffic or incompatible requests; operations gets opaque 5xx errors. | Synthetic probes, contract tests against provider stubs, timeout/error-rate dashboards by dependency, and configuration validation at startup. | 8 | 5 | 6 | 240 | Add provider contract tests, schema validation, explicit error mapping for 4xx/5xx/timeouts, bounded timeouts/circuit breakers, and versioned configuration with safe rollout. |
+
+## Priority actions before release
+
+1. Stop using H2 in-memory storage and fixture initialisation outside tests; verify backup and restore.
+2. Redesign payment processing around a durable state machine/outbox with idempotency and reconciliation for scheme and ledger ambiguity.
+3. Put mandatory fraud screening and an auditable fail-closed/override policy in front of scheme submission.
+4. Add concurrency-safe funds reservation and invariant monitoring for balance, daily limits, scheme instructions and ledger postings.
+5. Make queued-payment execution durable and observable, including retries, dead letters, replay and worker lag alerts.
+6. Complete and secure closure/case lifecycle workflows, and add a tenant/role/scope authorization matrix with negative tests.
+
+## Residual monitoring to require
+
+At minimum, alert on API 5xx/latency, authentication/authorization failures, due queued payments, old
+`RECEIVED`/`POSTING_FAILED` payments, negative balances, daily-limit breaches, missing fraud decisions,
+scheme-versus-ledger mismatches, notification failures, pending-closure and case SLA breaches, database
+row-count/schema changes, and cross-tenant authorization test failures. Every payment and state transition
+should carry a durable correlation ID through the API, scheme, ledger, fraud, notification, audit log and
+reconciliation records.
